    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe old rule computed `membershipLevel` directly. I replaced it with a two-stage points system, keeping each responsibility in its own small unit:\n\n**1. New `MembershipPointsFormatter`** (`util/MembershipPointsFormatter.java`) \u2014 computes `membershipPoints` from an owner's factors:\n- start at 0\n- `+2` when an email is present\n- `+1` when `namesakeCount` is 0\n- `+2` for a household of 3+ (`householdSize >= 3`)\n- `+3` for tenure over 365 days (reusing the existing `OwnerTenure` helper)\n\n**2. Rewrote `MembershipLevelFormatter`** \u2014 now has the single job of mapping points \u2192 level: 1 (0\u20131), 2 (2\u20133), 3 (4\u20135), 4 (6+).\n\n**3. `OwnerMapper`** \u2014 added a `membershipPoints` mapping and pointed `membershipLevel` at `MembershipLevelFormatter.format(MembershipPointsFormatter.format(...))`, so the points formula lives in exactly one place and the level is derived from it.\n\n**4. `openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `membershipPoints` schema (the `OwnerDto` is generated from this spec) and updated the `membershipLevel` description to reflect the new derivation.\n\n## Verification\n\n`./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The acceptance test (`membershipPoints=3`, `membershipLevel=2` for a fresh owner with email, no namesake, single household, zero tenure) passes.\n\nNote the household points read from `Owner.householdSize`, which already existed on the entity \u2014 that's the field I interpreted as \"household of 3 or more.\" If your spec intends household size to be derived differently, let me know.",
